      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, because the dose is unsafe as written.\n\nAcetaminophen 2000 mg three times daily is 6,000 mg/day. That is well above the usual adult maximum of 4,000 mg/day. Many institutions cap it at 3,000 mg/day. A single 2,000 mg dose is also double the usual maximum single dose of 1,000 mg. At this dose the risk of hepatotoxicity is serious, even with normal liver function.\n\nHere are some standard options for a 70 kg adult with normal hepatic and renal function:\n- **650 mg PO every 6 hours**, which is 4 times daily and 2,600 mg/day.\n- **1000 mg PO three times daily**, which is 3,000 mg/day.\n- **1000 mg PO four times daily**, which is 4,000 mg/day. This is the absolute ceiling, and many services prefer to stay lower.\n\nDid you mean 1000 mg three times daily, or was there a different intended dose or frequency? Please confirm with your supervising resident or attending as well. Once you tell me the dose, I'll enter it right away."
      }
     ],
