       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change the safety problem. 6,000 mg/day of acetaminophen is 50% over the 4,000 mg/day maximum, and each 2,000 mg dose is double the usual single-dose limit. It could cause serious liver injury in a patient with normal liver function. I'm not aware of any standard indication for it.\n\nIf you think there's a reason for this dose, here are some ways to proceed:\n- **Check with your senior resident, attending, or pharmacist.** If they agree after review, they can enter or co-sign it through the process your hospital uses for exceptions. Pharmacy will probably flag it anyway.\n- **Enter a standard dose now** so the patient's pain or fever is still treated. Options are 1000 mg PO three times daily (3,000 mg/day) or 650 mg PO every 6 hours (2,600 mg/day). I can enter either one right away.\n- **Consider other options if the pain control is inadequate.** Examples are a scheduled NSAID if appropriate, or a multimodal regimen. Your team can decide on these.\n\nWould you like me to enter one of the standard-dose options?"
